# DOCKER-USER policy, IPv4. Managed by bin/provision-host.sh and reconciled by
# agentic-postgres-docker-firewall.service after every docker.service start.
#
# Why this file exists at all: UFW filters the INPUT chain, and Docker-published
# ports are DNAT'd and traverse FORWARD. A port published by a container is
# therefore reachable from the Internet even when `ufw status` shows it denied.
# DOCKER-USER is the one chain Docker guarantees it will not overwrite, and it
# is consulted before Docker's own accept rules.
#
# --ctorigdstport, not --dport. This is the detail that makes or breaks the
# policy: DNAT has already rewritten the destination by the time a packet
# reaches FORWARD, so --dport 80 would match the *container's* port. The
# conntrack original destination port is the port the client actually asked
# for, which is what the policy is about.
#
# Each non-comment line is an iptables rule specification, appended to
# DOCKER-USER in this order. The launcher flushes only the rules it manages and
# re-adds them, so reconciliation is idempotent and does not disturb rules
# Docker or another operator put there.
#
# Substituted by provision-host.sh:
#   __PUBLIC_INTERFACE__   resolved from host.yaml, or from the default route
#                          when public_interface is `auto`. Ambiguity is
#                          refused rather than guessed.

# Return traffic for connections this host or its containers established.
# First, so the cost of the rest is only paid by new connections.
-i __PUBLIC_INTERFACE__ -m conntrack --ctstate RELATED,ESTABLISHED -j RETURN

# The only two ports the public Internet may open a new connection to. Both
# belong to Traefik; no project service publishes a host port.
-i __PUBLIC_INTERFACE__ -p tcp -m conntrack --ctstate NEW --ctorigdstport 80 -j RETURN
-i __PUBLIC_INTERFACE__ -p tcp -m conntrack --ctstate NEW --ctorigdstport 443 -j RETURN

# Everything else forwarded from the public interface toward a Docker network
# is dropped. This is what makes "no public route reaches the direct PostgreSQL
# endpoint" (SEC-NET-001) true by construction rather than by there being no
# PostgreSQL yet: when Session 4 adds one, publishing it by accident still
# reaches nothing.
#
# DROP rather than REJECT: a reject response confirms the host is filtering and
# tells a scanner the port exists but is blocked. Silence is a smaller answer.
-i __PUBLIC_INTERFACE__ -j DROP
